## Core Reflection Operations

```java
// Get Class object — multiple ways:
Class<String> c1 = String.class;          // Compile-time
Class<?> c2 = "hello".getClass();         // From instance
Class<?> c3 = Class.forName("java.lang.String");  // From name (dynamic)

// Inspect class structure:
Class<?> clazz = Order.class;
System.out.println(clazz.getName());         // com.example.Order
System.out.println(clazz.getSimpleName());   // Order
System.out.println(Modifier.toString(clazz.getModifiers())); // public

// Fields:
Field[] fields = clazz.getDeclaredFields();  // All fields (incl. private)
for (Field field : fields) {
    System.out.println(field.getName() + ": " + field.getType().getSimpleName());
}

// Access private field:
Field privateField = clazz.getDeclaredField("secretCode");
privateField.setAccessible(true);  // Bypass access control
Order order = new Order();
Object value = privateField.get(order);      // Read private field
privateField.set(order, "newValue");         // Write private field

// Methods:
Method[] methods = clazz.getDeclaredMethods();
Method processMethod = clazz.getDeclaredMethod("processOrder", Long.class, String.class);
processMethod.setAccessible(true);
Object result = processMethod.invoke(order, 42L, "EXPRESS");  // Invoke

// Constructor:
Constructor<Order> ctor = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor(String.class, Long.class);
Order newOrder = ctor.newInstance("Test", 99L);
```

## Practical Use: Simple Object Copier

```java
public class ObjectCopier {

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static <T> T shallowCopy(T source) {
        try {
            Class<T> clazz = (Class<T>) source.getClass();
            T copy = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();  // No-arg constructor

            for (Field field : clazz.getDeclaredFields()) {
                if (Modifier.isFinal(field.getModifiers())) continue;  // Skip final
                field.setAccessible(true);
                field.set(copy, field.get(source));
            }
            return copy;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to copy object", e);
        }
    }
}

// Usage in tests — copy to create independent test fixture
Order original = new Order("test", BigDecimal.TEN);
Order copy = ObjectCopier.shallowCopy(original);
copy.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);  // Doesn't affect original
```

## Performance — Direct vs Reflection

```java
// Performance comparison (approximate):
// Direct method call: ~1 ns
// Method.invoke() (first time): ~100 ns (class loading)
// Method.invoke() (cached): ~50 ns
// MethodHandle (via invokedynamic): ~5 ns (JIT-optimizable)

// Cache Method objects — don't look up every call:
public class CachingReflectionService {
    private static final Map<String, Method> methodCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public Object invoke(Object target, String methodName, Object... args) throws Exception {
        String key = target.getClass().getName() + "#" + methodName;
        Method method = methodCache.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> {
            try {
                return target.getClass().getDeclaredMethod(methodName, /* params */);
            } catch (NoSuchMethodException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        });
        return method.invoke(target, args);
    }
}

// Modern alternative — MethodHandle:
MethodHandles.Lookup lookup = MethodHandles.lookup();
MethodHandle handle = lookup.findVirtual(Order.class, "getStatus",
    MethodType.methodType(OrderStatus.class));
// handle is JIT-optimizable — much faster than Method.invoke() in loops
OrderStatus status = (OrderStatus) handle.invoke(order);
```

## Production Reality

In Spring applications, you rarely write reflection code directly — the framework handles it. You write the annotations and structure; Spring's reflection-based infrastructure does the work. The cases where you might use reflection: custom annotation processors, serialization utilities, test helpers that access private state, and framework extension points. Always benchmark reflection code if it's in a frequently-called path — the overhead matters at scale.

## Defining a Custom Annotation

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Target({
    ElementType.METHOD,      // Can annotate methods
    ElementType.TYPE         // Can annotate classes
})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)  // Available at runtime via reflection
@Documented                          // Included in Javadoc
public @interface RateLimited {
    int requestsPerMinute() default 60;
    String key() default "";  // Custom key for rate limit bucket
}

// Usage:
@RestController
public class ApiController {

    @GetMapping("/expensive-operation")
    @RateLimited(requestsPerMinute = 10, key = "expensive-op")
    public ResponseEntity<Result> expensiveOperation() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(service.doWork());
    }

    @PostMapping("/standard")
    @RateLimited  // Uses defaults: 60/min
    public ResponseEntity<Void> standardOperation() {
        service.doStandardWork();
        return ResponseEntity.ok().build();
    }
}
```

## Processing with Spring AOP

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class RateLimitingAspect {

    private final RateLimiterService rateLimiter;

    // Pointcut: any method annotated with @RateLimited
    @Pointcut("@annotation(rateLimited)")
    public void rateLimitedMethod(RateLimited rateLimited) {}

    @Around("rateLimitedMethod(rateLimited)")
    public Object enforceRateLimit(
            ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint,
            RateLimited rateLimited) throws Throwable {

        // Read annotation attributes:
        int requestsPerMinute = rateLimited.requestsPerMinute();
        String key = rateLimited.key().isEmpty()
            ? joinPoint.getSignature().toShortString()
            : rateLimited.key();

        // Apply rate limiting:
        if (!rateLimiter.tryAcquire(key, requestsPerMinute)) {
            throw new TooManyRequestsException(
                "Rate limit exceeded for: " + key);
        }

        return joinPoint.proceed();
    }
}
```

## Custom Validation Annotation

```java
@Target(ElementType.FIELD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = PhoneNumberValidator.class)
@Documented
public @interface ValidPhoneNumber {
    String message() default "Invalid phone number format";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}

public class PhoneNumberValidator
        implements ConstraintValidator<ValidPhoneNumber, String> {

    private static final Pattern PHONE_PATTERN =
        Pattern.compile("^\\+[1-9]\\d{1,14}$");  // E.164 format

    @Override
    public boolean isValid(String phone, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
        if (phone == null) return true;  // Use @NotNull for null check
        return PHONE_PATTERN.matcher(phone).matches();
    }
}

// Usage in DTO:
public class CreateUserRequest {
    @NotBlank
    private String name;

    @ValidPhoneNumber  // Custom validation
    private String phone;
}

// In controller with @Valid: Spring automatically runs all validators
@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest req) { ... }
```

## Reading Annotations via Reflection

```java
// Read class-level annotation:
if (OrderService.class.isAnnotationPresent(Service.class)) {
    Service annotation = OrderService.class.getAnnotation(Service.class);
    System.out.println("Service value: " + annotation.value());
}

// Read method annotations:
for (Method method : OrderService.class.getDeclaredMethods()) {
    if (method.isAnnotationPresent(RateLimited.class)) {
        RateLimited rl = method.getAnnotation(RateLimited.class);
        System.out.printf("Method %s: %d req/min%n",
            method.getName(), rl.requestsPerMinute());
    }
}

// Read parameter annotations:
for (Parameter param : method.getParameters()) {
    Annotation[] annotations = param.getAnnotations();
    for (Annotation ann : annotations) {
        System.out.println("Param annotation: " + ann.annotationType().getSimpleName());
    }
}

// Spring utility:
AnnotationUtils.findAnnotation(method, Transactional.class);
// Checks method AND its class hierarchy — handles @Transactional on class
```

## Production Reality

Custom annotations with AOP are the right tool for cross-cutting concerns: audit logging, rate limiting, caching, permission checks, method tracing. They keep business logic free of infrastructure concerns. The key is to make the annotation's semantics clear from its name and attributes — `@AuditLog(action = "CREATE_ORDER")` is self-documenting. Use Spring's `AnnotationUtils` for reliable annotation lookup — it handles inherited annotations and proxied classes correctly.

## Lombok APT — What Gets Generated

```java
// You write:
@Data
@Builder
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class Order {
    private Long id;
    private String status;
    private BigDecimal total;
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}

// Lombok APT generates at compile time:
// - Getters: getId(), getStatus(), getTotal(), getCreatedAt()
// - Setters: setId(), setStatus(), setTotal(), setCreatedAt()
// - equals(Object): based on all fields
// - hashCode(): based on all fields
// - toString(): "Order(id=1, status=PENDING, ...)"
// - Builder: Order.builder().id(1).status("PENDING").build()
// - Constructors: Order(), Order(id, status, total, createdAt)
// All type-checked at compile time — no runtime overhead!
```

## MapStruct APT — Type-Safe Mappers

```java
// You write the interface:
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface OrderMapper {
    OrderDTO toDTO(Order order);
    Order toEntity(CreateOrderRequest request);

    @Mapping(source = "user.email", target = "userEmail")
    @Mapping(source = "items", target = "itemCount", qualifiedByName = "itemsToCount")
    OrderSummaryDTO toSummaryDTO(Order order);

    @Named("itemsToCount")
    default int itemsToCount(List<OrderItem> items) {
        return items == null ? 0 : items.size();
    }
}

// MapStruct APT generates at compile time (approximately):
@Component
public class OrderMapperImpl implements OrderMapper {
    @Override
    public OrderDTO toDTO(Order order) {
        if (order == null) return null;
        OrderDTO dto = new OrderDTO();
        dto.setId(order.getId());
        dto.setStatus(order.getStatus());
        dto.setTotal(order.getTotal());
        // ... explicit field assignment — no reflection at runtime!
        return dto;
    }
}
// 100% explicit code — type-safe, IDE navigable, zero reflection overhead
```

## Custom Annotation Processor

```java
@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.AutoRegister")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_17)
@AutoService(Processor.class)  // Google AutoService — register in META-INF
public class AutoRegisterProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {

    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (TypeElement annotation : annotations) {
            for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(annotation)) {
                TypeElement classElement = (TypeElement) element;
                generateRegistrationCode(classElement);
            }
        }
        return true;  // True = claim these annotations (don't pass to other processors)
    }

    private void generateRegistrationCode(TypeElement classElement) {
        // Use JavaPoet library to generate source code:
        TypeSpec registrationClass = TypeSpec.classBuilder(
                classElement.getSimpleName() + "Registration")
            .addModifiers(Modifier.PUBLIC)
            .addMethod(MethodSpec.methodBuilder("register")
                .addStatement("Registry.register($T.class)", classElement)
                .build())
            .build();

        JavaFile javaFile = JavaFile.builder(
            processingEnv.getElementUtils().getPackageOf(classElement).toString(),
            registrationClass
        ).build();

        try {
            javaFile.writeTo(processingEnv.getFiler());
        } catch (IOException e) {
            processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(Diagnostic.Kind.ERROR, e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

In Spring Boot applications, embrace Lombok for reducing boilerplate (but understand what it generates — occasionally it causes issues with serialization or JPA entity mutation). Use MapStruct for entity-to-DTO mapping — it's dramatically better than ModelMapper (which uses reflection) for performance and type safety. The generated code is readable in the `target/generated-sources` directory — inspect it when debugging unexpected mapping behavior.
